UNIT f64_bits
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@b329a40412df cli/sir/canon.mjs — encode(x, f64).bits, the normative bit pattern every f64 comparison in every oracle runs on (SIR_SCHEMA canonical form, the NaN rule)
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG f64_bits(x: f64) -> text
ENVELOPE every IEEE-754 binary64 value: finite normals, subnormals, both zeros, both infinities, and NaN of any sign and
  payload. Non-f64 arguments are outside the envelope (the real code throws on them; see SCOPE).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Render an f64 as the 16 lowercase hexadecimal digits of its IEEE-754 binary64 encoding, with every NaN
  replaced by the one canonical quiet NaN — so that f64 equality in an oracle is text equality of this rendering.
BEHAVIOR
R1 Read x as its 64-bit IEEE-754 binary64 encoding taken as an unsigned integer: bit 63 the sign, bits 62..52 the biased
  exponent, bits 51..0 the fraction. The result is that integer written in base 16, most significant digit first, with
  lowercase digits a-f, left-padded with "0" to exactly 16 characters.
R2 Every NaN, whatever its sign bit and whatever its payload (quiet or signaling), yields "7ff8000000000000". This is the
  ONE place the result is not the input's own encoding. CITED FIDELITY package — the NaN rule (SIR_SCHEMA canonical form)
R3 No other value is normalized: -0 and +0 differ, the infinities keep their sign, subnormals keep their exact fraction,
  and no rounding or tolerance is applied. Two f64 values give the same result exactly when both are NaN or their
  encodings are identical.
TABLE SPECIALS — x -> result (R1 except row 1, which is R2)
  1  NaN (any sign, any payload)                     -> "7ff8000000000000"
  2  +0                                               -> "0000000000000000"
  3  -0                                               -> "8000000000000000"
  4  +Infinity                                        -> "7ff0000000000000"
  5  -Infinity                                        -> "fff0000000000000"
  6  smallest positive subnormal (2 to the -1074)     -> "0000000000000001"
  7  largest subnormal                                -> "000fffffffffffff"
  8  smallest positive normal (2 to the -1022)        -> "0010000000000000"
  9  largest finite                                   -> "7fefffffffffffff"
  10 1                                                -> "3ff0000000000000"
INVARIANT the result is always exactly 16 characters, each one of 0-9 or a-f.
INVARIANT the result never has the NaN exponent pattern (exponent bits all ones, fraction non-zero) except as "7ff8000000000000".
ORACLE value -> oracles/f64_bits.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 6b106e6ba0d9a2f538e0953da58f89ca3a821d79793a2df131e4b6de3d088019
NOTE the oracle carries NaN arguments only as the codec's NaN tag, so its single NaN vector cannot show a non-canonical
  payload or a negative sign; R2 for those NaNs is stated here and pinned in each language file's MECHANISM.
SCOPE IN: R1-R3 over every f64. OUT: the decimal display string v that encode also emits (a non-normative hint), and the
  real code's handling of non-f64 arguments — a non-number throws "not f64: ...", and a legacy codec number tag whose text
  is not a numeral is first converted to NaN (an encode-codec fact, not this unit's behavior).
PROVENANCE rederive@b329a40412df (rederive, the trust kernel) cli/sir/canon.mjs, functions f64bits + hex16 as reached through
  encode(x, {k: 'f64'}).bits (seam selfhost/seams/f64_bits.mjs). Cut 2026-10-26, REDERIVE_SELF_PLAN Tier 1 round 1.
